Consider a dynamical system whose state at time t is described not only by a configuration x_t in some state space X, but also by a structure S_t belonging to a space S of admissible structures (e.g., interaction networks, sets of components, or rule schemata), and by a rule r_t ∈ R that governs how S_t evolves, i.e., S_{t+1} = r_t(S_t, x_t). Suppose further that r_t itself evolves according to a meta-rule m_t ∈ M, with r_{t+1} = m_t(r_t, S_t, x_t), and that m_t may in turn evolve according to higher-order rules, yielding a hierarchy (x_t, S_t, r_t, m_t, m^{(2)}_t, …) of states, structures, and rules at successive levels.

Develop a practical mathematical and computational framework for modeling such hierarchies of evolutionary change, in which:
(a) changes to the rules generating structural change in the system can be represented and simulated;
(b) the framework supports an arbitrary (or at least extendable) number of meta-levels;
(c) it admits inference, prediction, or qualitative analysis of trajectories from observed data of biological or social systems undergoing transformation;
(d) it reduces, at the lowest level, to standard models of state evolution on a fixed structure with fixed rules.

Specifically, propose explicit constructions (for example based on category-theoretic, type-theoretic, stochastic-process, or open-ended evolutionary computation formalisms) and demonstrate that the resulting models can capture transformations that are not expressible as evolution under a fixed rule set on a fixed structure space.

